# Off-NAS copy of the Platform PostgreSQL backup spool (deploy/synology/scripts/offsite-postgres-backup.sh,
# runbook docs/operations/PLATFORM_POSTGRES_BACKUP_PITR.md "Off-NAS copy").
#
# Copy to a root-only file (chmod 0600) OUTSIDE the repository checkout and outside the backup spool, for example
# /volume1/docker/oteryn-offsite/offsite.env, and name it with OTERYN_OFFSITE_CONFIG. This file holds
# no secret: the repository URL, paths and settings only. The two secret files it names are root-only
# (chmod 0600) and never committed:
#   - the password file: the restic repository password (at least 32 random characters, e.g.
#     `head -c 48 /dev/urandom | base64`). It encrypts the copy. Keep a second copy OFF the NAS (password
#     manager or sealed escrow): if the NAS is lost, the copy cannot be read without it.
#   - the credentials file: KEY=VALUE provider credentials passed to restic as environment, scoped to the
#     destination bucket or path only, never the PostgreSQL credentials. Examples by destination:
#       S3 / S3-compatible: AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=...  AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=...  (AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=...)
#       Backblaze B2:       B2_ACCOUNT_ID=...  B2_ACCOUNT_KEY=...
#       Azure Blob:         AZURE_ACCOUNT_NAME=...  AZURE_ACCOUNT_KEY=...
#       REST server:        RESTIC_REST_USERNAME=...  RESTIC_REST_PASSWORD=...
#
# Destination, owner decision D-3 (2026-10-15): until production the copy stays ON the Synology NAS, in a
# restic repository on a separate shared folder (on another volume or disk group when the NAS has one),
# never inside the Docker data root or the backup spool. This is PRE-PRODUCTION and NOT disaster-proof:
# it survives a lost or corrupted database volume or spool, not the loss of the NAS. An off-NAS target
# (likely Backblaze B2) is chosen before production; the credentials file is needed only then.
# Off-NAS repository URL forms:
#   s3:https://s3.<region>.amazonaws.com/<bucket>/oteryn-platform-postgres
#   s3:https://<endpoint>/<bucket>/oteryn-platform-postgres      (S3-compatible: Wasabi, R2, Hetzner, ...)
#   b2:<bucket>:oteryn-platform-postgres
#   azure:<container>:/oteryn-platform-postgres
#   rest:https://<second-site-host>:8000/oteryn-platform-postgres  (restic rest-server, --append-only)
#   sftp:<user>@<second-site-host>:/backups/oteryn-platform-postgres
OTERYN_OFFSITE_REPOSITORY=/volume1/oteryn-backup-copy/restic/oteryn-platform-postgres
OTERYN_OFFSITE_PASSWORD_FILE=/volume1/docker/oteryn-offsite/secrets/restic-password
# Only for an off-NAS destination:
#OTERYN_OFFSITE_CREDENTIALS_FILE=/volume1/docker/oteryn-offsite/secrets/provider.env
# Local state: the last push result, the restic cache and the drill evidence. Not in the spool.
OTERYN_OFFSITE_STATE_DIR=/volume1/docker/oteryn-offsite/state

# Data-loss target of the copy, in seconds: `status` and `check` exit 3 (degraded) when the newest copy
# is older. 300 is the Platform RPO, owner decision D-2 (2026-10-15), equal to the Game database target
# (Game ADR 0004, operability review 2026-10-06).
OTERYN_OFFSITE_TARGET_RPO_SECONDS=300
# Retention of the snapshots at the destination (restic --keep-within-*). Every snapshot holds the whole
# spool, so each kept snapshot keeps point-in-time recovery over the 7 days of WAL before it.
OTERYN_OFFSITE_KEEP_WITHIN_HOURLY=48h
OTERYN_OFFSITE_KEEP_WITHIN_DAILY=14d
OTERYN_OFFSITE_KEEP_WITHIN_WEEKLY=56d
# Share of the stored data that `check` downloads and authenticates each run.
OTERYN_OFFSITE_CHECK_SUBSET=5%
# How long a restic run waits for a repository lock held by another run (forget, check).
OTERYN_OFFSITE_LOCK_RETRY=5m
# Optional: the spool volume when no postgres-backup container exists (default: found by Compose labels).
#OTERYN_OFFSITE_SPOOL_VOLUME=oteryn-staging_postgres_backup
